explain.narrative:
## Core concept
UNION ALL combines result rows from multiple SELECT statements and preserves every row, including duplicate rows. The SELECT branches must return the same number of columns with compatible data types in corresponding positions.

## Syntax
```sql
SELECT column_list FROM source_a
UNION ALL
SELECT column_list FROM source_b;
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Keep repeated visitor names
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_morning;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_evening;
CREATE TABLE visits_morning (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE visits_evening (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO visits_morning VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO visits_evening VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Bilal','Hyderabad');
SELECT visitor_name FROM visits_morning UNION ALL SELECT visitor_name FROM visits_evening ORDER BY visitor_name;
```
Expected result: Asha; Asha; Bilal; Bilal; Chitra; Dev

UNION ALL preserves both visits by Asha and both visits by Bilal.

### 2. Compare UNION and UNION ALL row counts
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_morning;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_evening;
CREATE TABLE visits_morning (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE visits_evening (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO visits_morning VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO visits_evening VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Bilal','Hyderabad');
SELECT COUNT(*) AS all_visit_rows FROM (SELECT visitor_name FROM visits_morning UNION ALL SELECT visitor_name FROM visits_evening);
```
Expected result: 6

The combined event list contains all six input rows.

### 3. Combine source labels with every event
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_morning;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_evening;
CREATE TABLE visits_morning (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE visits_evening (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO visits_morning VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO visits_evening VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Bilal','Hyderabad');
SELECT 'Morning' AS session, visitor_name FROM visits_morning UNION ALL SELECT 'Evening' AS session, visitor_name FROM visits_evening ORDER BY session, visitor_name;
```
Expected result: Evening | Asha; Evening | Bilal; Evening | Dev; Morning | Asha; Morning | Bilal; Morning | Chitra

The session label keeps each source context, while UNION ALL retains every row.

### 4. Filter each branch before combining
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_morning;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_evening;
CREATE TABLE visits_morning (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE visits_evening (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO visits_morning VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO visits_evening VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Bilal','Hyderabad');
SELECT visitor_name, city FROM visits_morning WHERE city = 'Hyderabad' UNION ALL SELECT visitor_name, city FROM visits_evening WHERE city = 'Hyderabad' ORDER BY visitor_name;
```
Expected result: Bilal | Hyderabad; Bilal | Hyderabad

Each branch is filtered independently and duplicate visitor-city rows remain.

### 5. Aggregate the combined event stream
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_morning;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visits_evening;
CREATE TABLE visits_morning (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE visits_evening (visit_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, visitor_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO visits_morning VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO visits_evening VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Bilal','Hyderabad');
SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS visit_count FROM (SELECT city FROM visits_morning UNION ALL SELECT city FROM visits_evening) AS all_visits GROUP BY city ORDER BY city;
```
Expected result: Hyderabad | 2; Mumbai | 1; Rajahmundry | 2; Vijayawada | 1

The derived table preserves all events before the outer query counts them by city.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite and use portable core SQL syntax. Column positions and compatible data types must align across set-operation branches. Result order is not guaranteed without an outer ORDER BY.

## Common mistakes
- Returning different numbers of columns from the SELECT branches.
- Putting columns in different semantic positions.
- Assuming UNION and UNION ALL treat duplicates the same way.
- Expecting output order without ORDER BY.
- Using incompatible types across corresponding columns.

## Best practices
Choose UNION when duplicate elimination is required and UNION ALL when every source row must be preserved. Align selected columns deliberately, add a source label when provenance matters, and sort only in the final combined query.

